WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.720
"How to Get the Ideal Ferritin Level
and Avoid Symptoms of Iron Deficiency"

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.640
As I discussed
in my last video,

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.880
iron can be
a double-edged sword.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:12.320
It's an essential mineral,

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.280
but simultaneously a danger
as a pro-oxidant;

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.680
so, our body tries
to regulate levels

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.200
but can really only do that

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.000
through altering
how much iron we absorb.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.440
We don't have a mechanism
to get rid of excess iron;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.600
so, it accumulates
because, like calories,

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.440
it was better evolutionarily

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.240
to have too much
rather than too little.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:35.920
So, as we age, our iron stores
get higher and higher.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.160
For men, it starts
immediately in adulthood,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.240
and women begin to catch up
after they stop menstruating.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.440
Remember, in my last video,

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:44.960
I explored
the evidence suggesting

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.720
that the ideal ferritin level—
the sweet spot—

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.280
is between 15 and 50,

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.560
but the average level
may be higher than that.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:55.840
Why would we evolve

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.800
to accumulate
potentially toxic levels?

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.360
Most of the iron in our bodies
is in our blood,

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.760
and we can lose it
in significant amounts

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:04.960
rather abruptly.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.120
And our ability
to make new blood from iron

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:10.080
is 20 times faster
than we can absorb it.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.200
Therefore, our ability
to store some excess iron

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.040
became
an evolutionary advantage,

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.880
allowing for rapid recovery

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.360
from an unexpected loss
of blood.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.000
Unfortunately,
that comes at a cost.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.200
Carrying excess iron
puts us at elevated risk

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.680
because of all the free radicals
it generates.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.120
So, what was once
a valuable survival mechanism

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:33.280
has become an anachronism.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:36.480
Today, we can replace lost blood
with a transfusion;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.920
so, we no longer have to keep
these high stores of iron.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.840
So, we're left with the risks
without the benefits.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:47.280
Now, if you're above that
15 to 50 ferritin sweet spot,

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.600
donating blood
is always an option—

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.160
a safe, feasible,
and inexpensive way

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:56.640
to rid yourself of excess iron,
and it's a mitzvah!

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.520
Or, you can reduce
your meat consumption

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.560
and lower iron levels that way
to gain health benefits,

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.000
especially if you aren't losing
blood naturally every month.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.240
Our body is better able
to regulate the absorption

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:11.040
of the proper amount
of the non-heme iron

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:12.640
concentrated in plant foods

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.080
rather than the heme iron

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.240
found predominantly
in blood and muscle.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:18.080
But you can go too low.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:20.880
The World Health Organization
defines low ferritin

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.040
as levels under 15,

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.760
and vegetarians are more likely
to fall below that,

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.760
especially menstruating women.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.160
But does having low iron stores
cause problems

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:31.680
if you don't become anemic—

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.480
meaning as long
as your blood counts are okay?

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.560
If you run
a social media poll,

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.400
iron-deficient women self-report
a wide range of symptoms,

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.720
but some are so nonspecific,
like fatigue,

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.960
it's hard to know
if low iron's the cause.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:47.520
What we need
is a study like this,

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.120
comparing the health
and well-being

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:49.920
of hundreds of women

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.320
who fall under
the ferritin level of 15,

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.000
with depleted iron stores,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.440
versus those with larger,
sufficient iron stores.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.160
The only significant difference
between iron-depleted

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.920
and iron-sufficient women
concerned memory disorders,

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.480
which were
significantly less common

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:07.920
in iron-depleted women.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.280
So, the iron-deficient women

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.120
appeared to have
better memory.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.120
Here's what
the researchers found.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:14.320
No significant difference

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.240
between low
and normal iron stores

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.640
when it came
to having palpitations,

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:20.960
their general feeling
about their health status,

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:22.400
their inability
to concentrate,

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:23.920
and sleeping disorders,

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:25.920
but there was
apparently better memory

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:27.680
among the iron depleted,

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.040
yet no significant difference

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.120
for vertigo, fainting,
nervousness,

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.800
thinned spoon-like fingernails,

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.120
or inflammation
of the lips or tongue.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:37.920
And no significant difference
was seen in physical health,

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.240
mental health, social health,
general health,

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:43.360
perceived health, self-esteem,
anxiety, depression,

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:44.800
pain, or disability.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:47.040
And finally,
no significant difference

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:48.480
in the rates of infections,

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:50.000
including
respiratory infections,

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:52.080
bladder infections,
other infections,

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.240
having to see a doctor
or other health professionals,

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.240
being in the hospital,
having to take sick leave,

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:57.600
or having a fever.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.640
In other words, there appears
to be no conclusive evidence

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.880
that an absence of iron stores
has negative consequences.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:07.040
But that's just
our backup iron stores,

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:08.240
our ferritin level.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:12.000
If our iron gets too low,
we can actually become anemic,

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.160
where we don't have enough iron
to make blood,

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:17.200
and that can have
serious consequences.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.800
Are vegetarians more likely
to become anemic?

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:24.160
And, regardless,
what's the best way to treat it?

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:27.994
I'll answer
those questions, next.

